1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,940
This is CanLab running against a real vehicle capture, not a
synthetic one.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:08,430
Twelve thousand nine hundred and seventy four frames,

3
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:13,238
one hundred and eighty distinct arbitration IDs, published
with SavvyCAN.

4
00:00:13,238 --> 00:00:19,297
The frame table fills, and a byte lights up when it changes
from the previous frame with the

5
00:00:19,297 --> 00:00:19,824
same ID.

6
00:00:19,824 --> 00:00:22,293
The panel on the left is the real bus:

7
00:00:22,293 --> 00:00:27,101
one hundred and eighty IDs, the busiest running at over one
hundred hertz.

8
00:00:27,101 --> 00:00:33,144
Selecting one opens it in the inspector, with the last frames
in hex and per-byte statistics.

9
00:00:33,144 --> 00:00:37,215
SIGNALS classifies all one hundred and eighty messages
offline.

10
00:00:37,215 --> 00:00:43,224
On a real bus this is the difference between a wall of hex and
a short list worth looking at.

11
00:00:43,224 --> 00:00:48,717
AUTO-RE sweeps every message for rolling counters and checksum
bytes.

12
00:00:48,717 --> 00:00:53,891
On this capture it finds twenty five counters and five
checksums,

13
00:00:53,891 --> 00:00:56,916
including CRC-8 in its SAE J1850 form.

14
00:00:56,916 --> 00:01:02,568
Testing only the simple sums, as it used to, found nothing at
all here.

15
00:01:02,568 --> 00:01:08,496
Per-bit entropy across the same real traffic suggests where
one field ends and the next begins:

16
00:01:08,496 --> 00:01:11,616
ninety two candidate fields across fifty messages.

17
00:01:11,616 --> 00:01:15,957
A signal is defined on the byte that moves most in the busiest
message,

18
00:01:15,957 --> 00:01:19,992
and the live preview decodes real frames through it straight
away.

19
00:01:19,992 --> 00:01:23,654
Plotted over time against the raw byte it came from.

20
00:01:23,654 --> 00:01:28,584
This is real vehicle data, so the shape is whatever the car
was doing.

21
00:01:28,584 --> 00:01:34,635
The heatmap shows how much each byte of each message moves
across all one hundred and eighty

22
00:01:34,635 --> 00:01:35,424
IDs at once.

23
00:01:35,424 --> 00:01:37,642
Periodicity across the real bus:

24
00:01:37,642 --> 00:01:43,464
eighty IDs are cyclic, and the tab also correlates bytes
between different messages.

25
00:01:43,464 --> 00:01:45,692
Everything so far only listened.

26
00:01:45,692 --> 00:01:52,165
Connected to a bus with ARM TX off, injection and diagnostics
were both exercised and nothing

27
00:01:52,165 --> 00:01:53,140
left the tool:

28
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:58,152
zero frames while disarmed, and the frame appears only once it
is armed.
